From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 12:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] script, libply-splash-core, renderers/drm: One eye on
 every screen a stereo boot does not convert

On a stereo boot (plymouth.stereo=) with a stereo theme every screen
showed what the script plugin's single window gave it: each display was
centred as one block in a window as wide as the widest display. With two
screens of different sizes (a 1366x768 laptop panel beside a 1920x1080
television) the narrower stereo display took its two eye halves from
places that are not the window's eyes: the panel's anaglyph mixed two
pictures 554 px apart, and a screen showing the left eye showed a crop.
A display that cannot take both eyes (the frame-buffer renderer, before
the GPU's driver) made Window.GetStereo () false for the whole window,
so a theme that laid itself out then stayed one picture when the DRM
screens arrived, and their left halves showed that picture with parts of
the right eye beside it.

Now the window holds both eyes whenever the boot is stereo and the
theme draws them, each eye the size of the main screen's (the first
output plymouth.stereo= names; the largest display while the main
screen is not there). A display that shows both eyes takes each of its
halves from the middle of its own eye of the window; a display that
shows one eye (not named in plymouth.stereo=, no stereo mode, or a
renderer without the conversion) takes the middle of the left eye. A
display larger than the eye continues the eye's own edge beside and
beyond it, each pixel the nearest pixel of the eye
(ply_pixel_buffer_fill_with_edges_of_area), so it shows no frame of
another colour and never a part of the other eye; the eye itself is
not scaled. A screen named with a mode it does not list keeps its own
mode and shows the left eye: no other 3D mode is chosen in its place.
Each of these is said once in Plymouth's log. Themes that do not
declare Stereo=full-side-by-side and boots without plymouth.stereo=
are unchanged.

Window.GetX () and Window.GetY () without a display index keep returning
the window's origin, 0, as since !368 (wang xinpeng), although on a stereo
window a display larger than the main screen's eye sits at a negative
offset. A theme lays itself out again with
Plymouth.SetDisplayHotplugFunction when screens come and go.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
